 "note": "The root box is correct: Pokorny p.1134 '3. u̯eis- to flow; poison' and Klein s.v. virus 'I.-E. base *weis-, to melt away, rot' give exactly Nourai's gloss. Buck (4.89) and Watkins add that the poison word *wisós (Skt viṣa, Av. viša, Gk ios, L. vīrus) may be a separate IE word, since evidence for 'flow' is meagre. Weaker branches: (1) Latin viscum 'mistletoe, birdlime' (not 'slime') probably belongs to *weyḱs-/*uiks- with Greek ixos, not to *weys- (Wiktionary, Pokorny 'perhaps'); (2) the Germanic weasel/bison words are only doubtfully connected (Watkins: 'more likely of obscure origin'; Klein: imitative *wis- 'break wind'); (3) English bison came via Latin and French, not directly from Germanic; (4) Persian bīš / Pahlavi bīš 'aconite' is most likely an Indic loan (Skt viṣā 'Aconitum ferox', Hindi biš; Laufer, Wiktionary) rather than an inherited descendant of Av. viša- (whose MP reflex is wiš). Nourai's reading bēš is a legitimate reading of Pahlavi byš (MacKenzie reads bīš). The chart's NOTE points to *dwey- for a homonym (MP bēš 'harm' < Av. ṭbaēšah-), which is a different word from the plant name. The JSON matches the printed page (checked against the page image); no extraction errors.",
